[%- macro implement_routing(var) -%]
Route by the **declared target** (QS-321 — never inferred from the
files you touch): re-run `python scripts/qs/context.py`. If `context.py`
exits non-zero / prints no JSON, or returns empty `labels` **and**
`title`, the `gh` lookup failed: **STOP** and print:

```text
Cannot pick the implement variant: the context.py / gh lookup failed — retry, or tell me which variant to run.
```

(If `issue` is null — the branch carries no `QS_<N>` issue number — say
so instead of suggesting a retry.) Do **not** print the label message or
commands below; then wait for the user as described in the last
paragraph. Otherwise take
`target`. `factory` → `[[ var ]] = implement-setup-task`; `product` →
`[[ var ]] = implement-task`. For any other value (empty — no or
ambiguous `target:*` label), **STOP** and print (substitute `{{issue}}`
with the issue number from `context.py` in the messages and commands
below):

```text
Cannot pick the implement variant: issue #{{issue}} has no single target:* label. Label it with exactly one of target:factory or target:product (see the commands below), or tell me which variant to run.
```

Print the commands below to the user as well. **Do NOT run any of them
yourself** — the target label is the user's declaration (QS-321). The
user runs exactly one:

```bash
gh issue edit {{issue}} --add-label target:factory
```

```bash
gh issue edit {{issue}} --add-label target:product
```

If `labels` already carries **both** target labels, tell the user to
remove the wrong one — `gh issue edit {{issue}} --remove-label target:factory`
or `gh issue edit {{issue}} --remove-label target:product` (the one that
is wrong; concrete labels only).

After printing (either STOP), wait for the user: if they say to retry or
report adding a label, re-run `context.py` and route again; if they name
`implement-task` / `implement-setup-task` (with or without a leading
`/`), or `factory` / `product` (map `factory` → `implement-setup-task`,
`product` → `implement-task`), use it (assign the bare name — drop any
leading `/`); any other answer → ask again.
[%- endmacro -%]
{#- QS-372: the phase handoff, written once for both harnesses. Call it as
    `[% call(prefix) m.handoff("<next_cmd>") %]` with banner lines
    (`[[ prefix ]] ...`) as the body. On Claude the user-facing block is the
    launcher's `handoff_text` (scripts/qs/launchers/claude.py), printed
    verbatim; on OpenCode the agent itself runs `new_context`, so its
    contract stays prose. `fix_plan` adds the review-fix flags and the
    existing-session block; `rerun` is the phase re-run after the fix. -#}
[% macro handoff(next_cmd, fix_plan=false, rerun="") %]
[% set banner = caller("✅" if harness == "claude" else "[OK]") | trim %]
**Before running** — substitute `{{worktree}}`, `{{issue}}`, `{{title}}`[[ ",\n`{{fix_plan_path}}`, `{{pr_number}}`" if fix_plan else "" ]]
with the values you captured earlier[[ (";\nsubstitute `" ~ next_cmd ~ "` with the value you resolved above") if next_cmd.startswith("{{") else "" ]].

```bash
python scripts/qs/next_step.py \
    --next-cmd "[[ next_cmd ]]" \
    --work-dir "{{worktree}}" \
    --issue {{issue}} \
    --title "{{title}}" \
[% if fix_plan %]
    --fix-plan-path "{{fix_plan_path}}" \
    --pr-number {{pr_number}} \
[% endif %]
    --harness [[ "claude-code" if harness == "claude" else "opencode" ]]
```

[% if harness == "claude" %]
Parse the JSON. If the command exits non-zero or prints no JSON, if the
JSON contains an `error` key, or if it has no `handoff_text`, STOP and
print the raw output verbatim. Otherwise print the report below,
replacing `<handoff_text>` with the payload's `handoff_text` **verbatim**
(do not rephrase, reorder or drop any part of it):

```text
[% if banner %]
[[ banner ]]

[% endif %]
<handoff_text>
[% if rerun %]

Then re-run /[[ rerun ]] (or open a fresh `claude --agent qs-[[ rerun ]]`
session) to verify.
[% endif %]
```
[% else %]
Parse the JSON output of ``next_step.py``.

**If `next_step.py` exits non-zero or prints no JSON, or its JSON has no
`new_context`**, STOP and print the raw output verbatim.

**If the `next_step.py` JSON contains an `error` key**, STOP and print
the raw JSON to the user. Do not proceed to run `new_context`.

[% if fix_plan %]
Otherwise capture the ``new_context`` string and the
``existing_session_prompt`` value. The ``existing_session_prompt`` is
a paste-into-already-running-session prompt — it is NOT a
session-spawn command. Do NOT execute it. **If
`existing_session_prompt` is missing or null, omit the "Already
running an implementation session?" block from the user report;
only emit it when the field is a non-empty string**.
[% else %]
Otherwise capture the ``new_context`` string.
[% endif %]

**Run `new_context` via the Bash tool**. The string is a
``python scripts/qs/spawn_session.py --agent qs-<phase> --directory
<wd> --title ... --prompt ...`` invocation — already inside the
allow-listed ``python scripts/qs/*`` pattern. Do NOT extract only the
prompt and send it to the current session. Do NOT strip
``--agent qs-<phase>``. The ``--agent`` flag is what binds the
next-phase orchestrator to the new session via OpenCode's HTTP API
``POST /session/<id>/prompt_async`` body — strip it and the prompt
lands on the default agent, breaking the pipeline silently.

**If the Bash tool returns an error before producing any JSON output**
(e.g., permission denied, missing interpreter), STOP and print the
Bash tool's error message verbatim. Do not attempt to parse JSON.

Parse the stdout of that command as JSON. The success contract is
**binary**:

- ``status == "session_created"`` AND ``agent`` equals `qs-` followed
  by the phase name passed to `--next-cmd` (here: `qs-[[ next_cmd ]]`)
  → success; report to the user:

  ```text
[% if banner %]
[[ banner | indent(2, first=true) ]]
[% endif %]
  [OK] Next phase session created: qs-[[ next_cmd ]]
       (visible in the OpenCode session list on the left)
[% if fix_plan %]

  Already running an implementation session?
  Paste this prompt into it:
    {{existing_session_prompt}}
[% endif %]
[% if rerun %]

  Then re-activate `qs-[[ rerun ]]` (or open a fresh session bound to
  it) to verify.
[% endif %]
  ```
[% if fix_plan %]

  (Omit the "Already running an implementation session?" block when
  `existing_session_prompt` is missing or null.)
[% endif %]

- **Anything else** (any other ``status`` value, missing or mismatched
  ``agent`` field, non-zero exit code, malformed JSON) → STOP. Print
  the raw JSON output verbatim to the user. Do NOT claim the next
  phase started. The user inspects the JSON and acts on the specific
  failure mode (``agent_file_missing``, ``agent_file_unreadable``,
  ``agent_file_empty``, ``worktree_invalid``, ``fallback_cli``,
  ``fallback_unavailable``, ``session_orphaned`` — each documented in
  ``scripts/qs/spawn_session.py``).
[% endif %]
[% endmacro %]
{#- #407: the review-session contract the two review agents share (D6, D14). `kind` is `global` or
    `cross_run`; the facts come from the launch prompt, never from this file. -#}
[% macro review_session(kind) %]
[% set source = "global_review" if kind == "global" else "cross_run_review" %]
## The facts

Your launch prompt carries every fact you need: your review id and kind,
the scope (one line per task: task, run, title, branch, `base_sha`,
`head_sha`, `head_state`), the exact `cp.py` command prefix, your `in/`
directory, your session token and your deadline. Never guess one of them.

## The commands you may run

Exactly six, always as `<prefix> <command words> …`, `<prefix>` being the
launch prompt's `<python> <MAIN>/scripts/qs/cp.py`:

| command | what for |
|---|---|
| `snapshot --run <R>` | a run: its `global_plan`, decisions, reports, `alerts` |
| `task show --task <T>` | one task: digest, criteria, ledger, convergence |
| `ledger show --task <T> --family --state open,rejected,settled,deferred` | what was already raised |
| `review read diff\|show\|grep\|ls-tree\|files …` | the code, at the shas of your prompt |
| `finding open … --token <token>` | your findings, one call per task |
| `review finish --input <file> --token <token>` | your hand-back, once |

**The command form.** Use only these six commands, and `Read` / `Write`
inside your `in/` directory; never run anything else, never edit a file
outside `in/`.

- One command per Bash call: no pipe, no `&&`, no `;`, no redirect, no
  `$(…)` or backticks, no environment prefix, no heredoc.
- Always `<prefix> <command words> …`, with `--token` (only on
  `finding open` and `review finish`) after the command words.
- `snapshot`, `task show`, `ledger show` and `review read` take no
  `--token`.

## Steps

1. Read `snapshot --run <R>` for each run of the scope (its
   `runs.global_plan`, decisions, reports, `alerts`), then
   `task show --task <T>` for each scope task. A task whose run is `-`
   has no run: skip `snapshot --run` for it.
2. Read `ledger show --task <T> --family --state open,rejected,settled,deferred`
   and raise nothing already there: point at it with `relates_to` instead.
3. Read the code only with `review read`, at the shas of your prompt:
   `review read diff --base <base_sha> --head <head_sha>` for the task's
   own change, `review read show --rev <head_sha> --path <file>`,
   `review read grep --rev <head_sha> --pattern '<regex>'`,
   `review read ls-tree --rev <head_sha>`. If an answer has
   `truncated: true`, list the changed files with
   `review read files --base <base_sha> --head <head_sha>` and read them
   one by one with `--path`. A truncated `files` answer: narrow it with
   `--path <dir>`. A task whose `base_sha` is `-` has no merge
   base: read it with `ls-tree` / `show` at `head_sha`. Never assume a
   working tree: you have none. A task with no head (`head_state` `plan` or
   `unresolved`) is reviewed from its plan (digest, criteria).

   `--pattern` is a basic regular expression (git's BRE): never escape
   `(`, `)`, `|`, `+`, `?`, `{` or `}` (`\(`, `\{`, `\|`, `\+`, `\?` are
   operators); `.`, `*`, `[`, `^`, `$` and `\` are special — escape one
   with `\` to match it. Pass the pattern in single quotes
   (`--pattern 'f(x)'`): unquoted, the shell eats a `\`, globs a `*` and
   fails on a `(`; a pattern holding `'` cannot be single-quoted: pick
   another one. A pattern starting with `-`: use the `=` form,
   `--pattern='--bg'` (with a space, argparse reads `--bg` as an option:
   `USAGE`). A pattern git cannot compile comes back
   `NOT_FOUND` with git's message: fix the pattern and retry once (the one
   exception to the `NOT_FOUND` rule below).

4. The lens:
[% if kind == "global" %]
   - does each task fit the run's global plan;
   - do the tasks fit together (contracts, shared code, order);
   - each deliverable's blast radius at its `head_sha`: `ok`, `doubt` or
     `too_large`.
[% else %]
   - do the runs' tasks conflict: the same code, the same feature built
     twice, contradictory decisions;
   - what must each run know about the others.
[% endif %]
5. For each finding, write a JSON file in your `in/` directory (one
   object or a list of them: `severity`, `category`, `title`, `body`, and
   optionally `file`, `symbol`, `line_start`, `line_end`, `relates_to`),
   then call
   `finding open --task <T> --phase <build if head_state is branch or merged, else plan> --source [[ source ]] --input <file> --token <token>`,
   one call per task[% if kind == "cross_run" %] (a cross-run finding is opened on each task it
   touches)[% endif %].
6. Write the finish file in your `in/` directory, call
   `review finish --input <file> --token <token>` once, then stop:

   ```json
[% if kind == "global" %]
   {"summary": "…", "tasks": {"T3": {"outcome": "pass", "blast_radius": "ok", "reason": "…"}, "T7": {"outcome": "fail", "reason": "…"}}}
[% else %]
   {"summary": "…", "tasks": {"T3": {"outcome": "pass"}, "T9": {"outcome": "fail", "reason": "…"}}}
[% endif %]
   ```

   Every scope task appears exactly once; `outcome` is `pass` or `fail`,
   and a `fail` needs its `reason`.
[% if kind == "global" %]
   `blast_radius` is required for every deliverable with a `head_sha`,
   and given on no task without one.
[% else %]
   A cross-run review writes no `blast_radius`: only the global review
   rates it.
[% endif %]

## Errors

- `USAGE` from `finding open` or `review finish`: fix the input and
  retry, at most twice.
- `CONFLICT`: do not retry that item; mention it in your summary.
- `NOT_FOUND`: do not retry; drop that read (or the `relates_to`) and
  continue.
- `INTERNAL`: retry once, then note it in your summary.
- `STALE_TOKEN`: stop at once — the review is closed or superseded.

## Hard rules

- Never edit, commit, push, post messages, or call another command or
  tool: your only writes are `finding open` and `review finish`.
- Read code only through `review read`, at the `base_sha` / `head_sha`
  of your prompt.
- Write files only in your `in/` directory.
- Your source is always `[[ source ]]`.
[% endmacro %]
